## Comparisons

Pinecone's comparisons with the systems teams usually weigh it against, plus guides that cover the major products in one area. Each page says where the other option is the better choice and cites that vendor's documentation.

- [Compare Pinecone (hub)](https://www.pinecone.io/compare/md/)
- [Pinecone vs pgvector](https://www.pinecone.io/compare/pgvector/md/): The index has to fit in memory. By default, filters run after the vector search has picked its candidates.
- [Pinecone vs Elasticsearch](https://www.pinecone.io/compare/elasticsearch/md/): On a cluster you run, you plan the shards and replicas yourself. Storage and compute scale together.
- [Pinecone vs OpenSearch](https://www.pinecone.io/compare/opensearch/md/): On a managed domain, you size node memory for the vector index and fix the shard count before you load data.
- [Pinecone vs S3 Vectors](https://www.pinecone.io/compare/s3-vectors/md/): Every query is billed on the size of the whole index, however little of it the query reads.
- [Pinecone vs MongoDB Vector Search](https://www.pinecone.io/compare/mongodb-atlas/md/): The index has to fit in RAM. Memory, query throughput, and tenant isolation are bought as one unit.
- [Pinecone vs Vertex AI Vector Search](https://www.pinecone.io/compare/vertex-ai/md/): On the classic Vector Search product you size and pay for the nodes that serve your index. Heavy filtering adds shards and the nodes to run them.
- [Pinecone vs Chroma](https://www.pinecone.io/compare/chroma/md/): Without Chroma Cloud, running Chroma in production means sizing and operating it yourself. A single node slows sharply once the index outgrows memory.
- [Best Knowledge Engine Platforms in 2026](https://www.pinecone.io/compare/best-knowledge-engine-platforms/md/) (2026-09-14)

